--- (4) CANDIDATE LAWS (weighted LS in linear Phi; weights 1/SE^2) ---
    fit points: SPARC bins (non-empty, SE>0) + O01 primary + O01 deep tail, N = 14
    (a) Phi = c x^p :            c = 2.679 +- 0.165,  p = +0.261 +- 0.029  (z_p = +8.98),  chi2 = 149.0/12 = 12.41
    (b) Phi = 1 + alpha ln x :   c0 = 2.547 +- 0.142,  alpha = +0.454 +- 0.058  (z_alpha = +7.77),  chi2 = 150.8/12 = 12.57
    (c) Phi = (1+x)/(1+x/b) :    b = 27867387.940 +- 17663513530257.031  (family implies Phi(1) = 2.000; the constraint Phi(1)=1 forces b=1, the constant Phi=1, degenerate), chi2 = 223.3/13 = 17.18
    (c2) Phi = c_b(1+x)/(1+x/b), Phi(1)=1:  b = 0.503 +- 0.034  (deep Phi(0) = 1.495, Phi(inf) = 0.751),  chi2 = 264.3/13 = 20.33

--- (5) VERDICT (pre-registered rule) ---
    C1: 22.2% of 9 overlapping comparisons within 3 combined SE (need >= 2/3): FAIL
    C2: power-law exponent p/SE_p = +8.98 (need |z| > 5): PASS
    VERDICT: The sign-mirror is a GENUINE SYSTEMATICS DIVIDE (registered finding): Phi(x) does NOT collapse across surveys at fixed x -- the same conclusion N05's arbitration program would reach, now data-side.
    BEST FIT: form (a);  Phi(x) = 2.679 x^+0.261  [p = +0.261 +- 0.029, chi2/dof = 12.41; fit on SPARC bins + O01 dwarfs, inverse-variance weighted].  Falsifier: any survey with >= 3 rings in one x-bin whose mean Phi departs > 3 combined SE from this curve (e.g., a MIGHTEE-class measurement at x ~ 0.03-0.1 that does not sit on Phi(x)); or a flat fit p with |p|/SE_p < 5 on the pooled SPARC+dwarf dataset.

